Subject: [PATCH 08118/11362] [outer-loop] Convert the inert-defer result to
 real benchmark units: +0.18 total, and the task premise's score formula was
 wrong

The premise converted an action cut with min((baseline/agent)^2, 115). The INSTALLED
scorer (arc_agi.scorecard, what the gateway runs) is min((baseline/agent)^2 * 100, 115)
per LEVEL, unsolved levels score 0.0, and the GAME score is an index-weighted mean
clamped to max_weights/total_weights*100. Two things the premise omits:

* A game can never score above what human-speed play on the levels it solved would have
  scored. Superhuman efficiency is worth exactly ZERO as a bonus; efficiency only pays as
  COMPENSATION for being slower than human.
* DEPTH is the dominant lever, not speed. So "an x% action cut is worth ~2x% on score" is
  not a general conversion.

Computed with EnvironmentScoreCalculator driven exactly as arc_leaderboard_eval.py does,
using the human baselines the ENV ITSELF exposes (ft09 L1 = 43 actions, lp85 = 17,
su15 = 22) rather than an assumed number:

  game    ctrl    defer    delta
  ft09  0.0000   0.1344  +0.1344
  lp85  0.0000   0.0338  +0.0338
  su15  0.0000   0.0120  +0.0120
  sp80/tu93/vc33 identical in both arms
  TOTAL 0.7445   0.9247  +0.1802     roster mean 0.0298 -> 0.0370 (1.242x)

The honest reading: this is a DEPTH gain, which is the right lever -- three games go from
0 levels to 1 inside the live budget, and an unbanked level scores 0 however few actions
went into it. It is NOT a speed gain in the scoring sense. The agent is still 6.0x / 9.1x
/ 13.6x SLOWER than human on the levels it banks, capturing 2.8% / 1.2% / 0.5% of what
solving that same single level at human speed would have scored. The 59.5x/9.0x/2.5x
per-level ratios are ratios between two arms of a very slow agent, not distance to the
frontier.

Still not significant (k=3 discordant, min reachable p = 0.25). Offline public-game
development proxy; the gateway charges RESET as an action and the offline harness does
not, so offline efficiency is optimistic.

